"**Irish ports with 10\u202f000\u202f\u2013\u202f100\u202f000 cruise\u2011passenger visits in *both* 2022 and 2023**\n\n| Port | 2022 passengers | 2023 passengers |\n|------|-----------------|-----------------|\n| **Killybegs** | 11\u202f146 | 27\u202f910 |\n| **Waterford** | 21\u202f276 | 17\u202f054 |\n\n**Why these two ports?**\n\nThe Central Statistics Office\u2019s \u201cStatistical of Port Traffic Q4 and Year\u202f2023\u201d table (Table\u202f6) lists cruise\u2011ship calls and the total number of passengers that called at each Irish port for 2022 and 2023.  \nFrom the table:\n\n- **Killybegs** \u2013 11\u202f146 passengers in 2022 and 27\u202f910 passengers in 2023\u3010commentary 1\u3011.  \n- **Waterford** \u2013 21\u202f276 passengers in 2022 and 17\u202f054 passengers in 2023\u3010commentary 1\u3011.  \n\nBoth ports fall within the 10\u202f000\u202f\u2013\u202f100\u202f000 range for each of the two years, while the other ports in the table either stayed below 10\u202f000 (e.g., Bantry Bay, Dublin, Galway) or exceeded 100\u202f000 in 2023 (e.g., D\u00fan\u202fLaoghaire, Cork). Thus, Killybegs and Waterford are the only Irish ports that meet the specified passenger\u2011visit threshold in both 2022 and 2023."